think Read REMEMBER (huge living card; extracted custody-gate facts by grep — gates #1 seed force-push, #2 hardware/wire, #3 funds, #4 real Kumara), the 1,024-round itinerary, and the six-season double-seat. Living edge is the AMUS constel consensus ladder, at AMUS12 (replicated log, no fork). The one rung the README names next — the socket — crosses the Comlink seam (seed/comlink/) and is custody gate #2, not agent-doable.
think Lindy-first crux-first among agent-doable rungs: AMUS12's log can never fork ONLY while the roster is fixed — every rung from AMUS9 reads sb.size() once as a constant. Membership reconfiguration is the single hardest still-pure consensus problem and a real blind spot the log left open. It needs no wire, no socket — it is a quorum-safety property, so it is agent-doable now and durable (the Mycelium consensus the tree is building will need it).
think Designed AMUS13 reconfig.rye around JOINT CONSENSUS: a transition from C_old to C_new commits only with a strict majority in BOTH configs. Spends the AMUS9 intersection lemma (2·threshold > n) directly — because any two strict majorities of a config intersect, a joint decision overlaps any pure-old and any pure-new decision, so the reconfiguration inherits the no-fork guarantee rather than reopening it.
think Matched the module shape to quorum.rye/decree.rye exactly: opening triad, ReconfigError with named refusals (NoJointMajority, DoubleVote, NotInEitherConfig, EmptyConfig), Config as a bounded membership mask + population (EmptyConfig at construction — a majority of nobody is undefined), JointConfig old/new, JointBallot.cast/seal/in_decision with ≥2 invariant-commented asserts each, require_joint the hard gate, run_reconfig driving a real round over the switchboard (every union member respond+hear interleaved at the naming-law border before its vote).
think The AMUS13 crux proof is enumerated directly, as AMUS10 checked leader uniqueness across every arrangement: over slots {0,1,2,3} with old {0,1,2} and new {1,2,3}, every joint-majority yes-set intersects every pure-old majority inside old and every pure-new majority inside new — no fork across the switch, shown exhaustively via masked popcounts rather than argued.
